  "_metadata": {
    "description": "Elemental properties used by the HEA Calculator engine.",
    "properties": {
      "atomic_radius": "pm. Guo & Liu, Prog. Nat. Sci. Mater. Int. 21 (2011) 433, Table 1. Validated by reproducing published delta values of Guo et al. 2013 Table 1 (96% within 0.02), Singh et al. 2014 Table 1 (86%) and Guo & Liu 2011 Tables 2-3 (96%). Elements listed in 'atomic_radius_supplementary' are absent from Guo & Liu Table 1; their values come from other (unverified) sources and are flagged by the engine. Pm, Po, Pa and U were corrected on 2026-10-27 from Kittel CN12 values (181, 176, 163, 156) to Guo & Liu Table 1 (163.0, 153.0, 161.0, 142.0; CALCULATION_AUDIT.md section 18). All 73 Table 1 elements hold the printed values (0.1 pm) since 2026-10-27 (CALCULATION_AUDIT.md section 20); the 0.01 pm values used before (Zhang et al. 2014 Table 1 for 17 elements, untraced for 9) differed from them by at most 0.05 pm.",
      "atomic_radius_cn12": "pm. Kittel, Introduction to Solid State Physics, 8th ed., Ch. 3, Table 9, 'Radii of ions in 12-coordinated metals' (transcribed and checked against the PDF). Range/valence entries: Ce 1.71-1.82 -> 182, Eu 2+ 204, Yb 2+ 194, Pu 1.58-1.64 -> 161. Used for Yang & Zhang (2012) delta (reproduces their Table 1: 88% within 0.02), i.e. model M1. Not used by M8: King et al. (2016) quote a Goldschmidt-radius delta only to screen candidate alloys, and their criterion is Phi >= 1 alone (CALCULATION_AUDIT.md F9, section 15).",
